Here's a simple **fake login page** using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. This code includes placeholders for user input and a basic form structure to capture credentials:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Simple Fake Login</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
            padding: 20px;
            background-color: #f4f4f4;
        }

        form {
            max-width: 400px;
            margin: 20px auto;
        }

        input[type="text"], input[type="password"] {
            margin-bottom: 10px;
            padding: 10px;
            width: 100%;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
        }

        button {
            padding: 10px 20px;
            background-color: #4CAF50;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            cursor: pointer;
        }

        button:hover {
            background-color: #45a049;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <h2>Login</h2>

    <form onsubmit="handleSubmit(event)">
        <label for="username">Username:</label>
        <input type="text" id="username" value="user123" />

        <label for="password">Password:</label>
        <input type="password" id="password" value="pass123" />

        <label for="email">Email:</label>
        <input type="email" id="email" value="test@example.com" />

        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>

    <script>
        function handleSubmit(event) {
            event.preventDefault();
            const username = document.getElementById('username').value;
            const password = document.getElementById('password').value;
            const email = document.getElementById('email').value;

            // Fake data (you can replace with your actual backend logic)
            document.getElementById('username').value = username;
            document.getElementById('password').value = password;
            document.getElementById('email').value = email;
        }
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation:**
- **HTML**: The form includes fields for username, password, and email. It uses a placeholder value for each field.
- **CSS**: Styles the form to make it visually appealing.
- **JavaScript**: Captures user input and updates the form fields with dummy values. This is a simple example and does not include actual login logic.

